WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.100
Two threads each add one to their own counter, 25 million times.

2
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.369
The card thread only writes card.

3
00:00:09.369 --> 00:00:11.637
The SEPA thread only writes sepa.

4
00:00:11.833 --> 00:00:15.595
They share no data, so there is nothing to lock, and no race.

5
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.165
Yet in one run, this took 1,220 ms.

6
00:00:19.165 --> 00:00:24.645
With the two fields padded apart, the same work took 247.

7
00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:27.856
The reason is in the hardware.

8
00:00:27.856 --> 00:00:31.248
Your CPU never reads one long from memory.

9
00:00:31.248 --> 00:00:34.883
It moves a whole cache line, 64 bytes on x86.

10
00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:36.972
This object is 32 bytes.

11
00:00:36.972 --> 00:00:43.322
After a 12-byte header and a 4-byte gap, card sits at offset 16, and sepa at 24.

12
00:00:43.322 --> 00:00:46.180
Both counters live on the same line.

13
00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:53.135
Each core has its own L1 and L2 caches, small fast memories right next to it.

14
00:00:53.135 --> 00:00:57.468
Core 1 runs the card thread, and core 2 runs the SEPA thread.

15
00:00:57.468 --> 00:00:59.953
Both cores keep a copy of the line.

16
00:01:00.633 --> 00:01:04.433
To write to a line, a core needs the only valid copy.

17
00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:08.519
So when core 1 writes card, core 2's copy is invalidated.

18
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:12.273
Core 2's next write has to take the line back first.

19
00:01:12.273 --> 00:01:14.815
Now core 1's copy is the invalid one.

20
00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:20.309
The line bounces between the cores on almost every write, and both threads slow down.

21
00:01:20.533 --> 00:01:21.997
This is false sharing.

22
00:01:21.997 --> 00:01:23.794
The program shares nothing.

23
00:01:23.794 --> 00:01:25.524
The hardware shares a lot.

24
00:01:26.333 --> 00:01:31.665
The fix is to keep at least 64 bytes between fields that different threads write.

25
00:01:31.665 --> 00:01:34.496
Padding pushes sepa onto a line of its own.

26
00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:36.839
Now each core keeps its own line.

27
00:01:36.839 --> 00:01:39.736
Both threads write, and no line has to move.

28
00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:46.855
On the course's shared 4-vCPU VM, one run showed a clear gap in all three rounds.

29
00:01:47.067 --> 00:01:53.039
But an earlier, more careful JMH experiment on the same VM could not show any effect.

30
00:01:53.039 --> 00:02:01.331
If the two threads land on hyperthreads of one physical core, they share its L1 and L2, so there is no line to bounce.

31
00:02:01.533 --> 00:02:03.114
The mechanism is certain.

32
00:02:03.114 --> 00:02:07.477
The size of the effect is not, so measure on the hardware you run on.

33
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.669
And every count still ends at exactly 25 million.

34
00:02:11.669 --> 00:02:14.784
False sharing costs time, never correctness.
